WHEREIS                        OS-9/68000                        WHEREISNAME     whereis -- find a program's source, command and documentationSYNOPSIS     whereis [-sbmu] [-SBM dir ... -f] name ...DESCRIPTION     whereis looks for each name where source, commands and documentation     are kept, and prints all it finds on one line, source first:       $ whereis gen       gen: /dd/SRC/divutils/gen.c /dd/SRC/divutils/gen.r /dd/SRC/flex/gen.c /dd/CMDS/gen     The places are       source          SRC       commands        CMDS, DEFS and LIB       documentation   DOC     on /dd, and on each other device that begins an entry in PATH -- so     with /h1/CMDS on PATH, /h1's CMDS, DEFS, LIB, SRC and DOC are searched     too.  Each directory is searched all the way down; a directory whose     own name matches is printed and not entered.     A name loses any leading directories and one trailing extension, so     `gen.c' looks for gen.  It then matches a file or directory called     name, or name followed by digits, either of them with one extension,     and an SCCS `s.' file of any of those.  Case does not matter, as it     does not to RBF.OPTIONS     -s, -b, -m          look only for source, commands or documentation; they combine     -u   print only the unusual names -- those without exactly one entry          of each kind looked for     -S dir ... -f,  -B dir ... -f,  -M dir ... -f          search these directories, all the way down, instead of the          usual ones for that kind; -f ends the listSEE ALSO     which, about, findORIGIN     whereis.c 5.5 (Berkeley) 4/18/91, from 4.3BSD Net/2.  The OS-9 version     searches OS-9's directories, all the way down, on each device PATH     names.  Both sources are in SRC/whereis.     This product includes software developed by the University of     California, Berkeley and its contributors.     Copyright (c) 1980 The Regents of the University of California.     All rights reserv